  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>In the heart of Sainte-Maxime, one specific sector of the town-centre beach, by the Pont du Préconil, is the only part where dogs are accepted — it is the real town-centre "dog beach" of Sainte-Maxime.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the official FAQ of the Sainte-Maxime tourist office (sainte-maxime.com, 2026), two areas of the town's coastline are open to dogs year-round: this town-centre dog beach (Pont du Préconil area) and the land part of the <a href="/en/fiche-pointe-des-sardinaux-sainte-maxime">Pointe des Sardinaux</a> (except in July-August). On all the other beaches of the town — the rest of the town centre, Les Oursins, La Nartelle, La Garonnette, La Madrague, Les Éléphants, La Croisette, and the southern tip of the Pointe des Sardinaux — dogs are not allowed year-round. A leash is compulsory and droppings must be picked up; only guide and assistance dogs are admitted on all beaches without exception.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: a widely distributed "Dog friendly" guide for the Gulf of Saint-Tropez claims a tolerance exists from October to May at La Croisette, Les Éléphants and La Madrague — but the FAQ and official beach pages on sainte-maxime.com, updated in 2026, mention no seasonal exception: the ban is presented as valid year-round. If in doubt, check the signage on site before settling in with your dog outside the two dedicated areas.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>A fenced dog park, the Jardin des chiens des Virgiles (chemin des Virgiles), is listed as free and open 24/7, limited to 10 dogs, in the 2025 "Dog friendly" leaflet from the Ramatuelle tourist office. Small dogs under 10 kg are admitted on ZOU buses, on the lap or in a basket (Sainte-Maxime tourist office FAQ).</p>
    </div>

